(*
Issue #203's own repro: `var v: integer value [1:[1: ... :0]]` at ~30,000
levels reliably overflowed the real C++ stack before this fix -- an
arm's value recurses straight back into parseComponentValue, and that
cycle (parseComponentValue <-> parseValueArms <-> parseVariantPartValue
<-> parseFieldListValue, EP §6.8.7) was the one recursive parser cycle
Parser.h's four pre-existing depth guards never wired a ceiling onto.
30,000 levels is well past this machine's own measured crash threshold
for this construct (empirically: 5,000-10,000 still parsed fine before
this fix; ~20,000+ reliably overflowed the stack, matching the issue's
own report). Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time --
lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic;
nobody should have to look at 30,000 levels of '[1:' to read this file,
so the lit directives come first.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero
exit code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the
two: it fails the whole step if the wrapped command crashes via signal,
rather than treating a crash as just another kind of "the inverted
command succeeded". So the step below only passes if the compiler exits
via a controlled, non-crashing diagnostic -- proving this is a real fix,
not just a report that happens to also be a nonzero exit.

RUN: not %plang -std=iso10206 %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

